I am making a simple program in which I have a class called Dice which contains a constructor, a method called roll(), and a toString() method. My problem is with the roll() method. I want it to return a random number from 1 to 6. However, when I run it, I keep getting the error: "The method roll() is undefined for the type Dice." What am I doing wrong?

    public class Dice {
        public Dice() {
        }

        public int roll() {
            return (int)(Math.random() * 6) + 1;
        }

        public String toString() {
            return "You rolled a " + roll() + ".";
        }

    }

    public class DiceDemo {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            Dice dice = new Dice();
            System.out.println(dice);
        }
    }
• What's the definition of the roll method in the Dice class? – Hovercraft Full Of Eels Oct 27 '16 at 23:14
• The code is shown in the question. – user3297925 Oct 27 '16 at 23:15
• No, it's not. You have shown the declaration of the method, but not the definition. – Hovercraft Full Of Eels Oct 27 '16 at 23:16
• I think he means the method body – Jashaszun Oct 27 '16 at 23:17
• Yes, that's what I meant. – user3297925 Oct 27 '16 at 23:17

You're trying to call the roll method in the toString method, which isn't valid because you haven't defined it yet. You need to put the body of the roll method below the declaration, and then it will work.

public class Dice {
public Dice() {
}

public int roll() {
return (int)(Math.random() * 6) + 1;
}

public String toString() {
return "You rolled a " + roll() + ".";
}
}


• That's what I thought, but I have a feeling that I need to define it in the main method. – user3297925 Oct 27 '16 at 23:17
• You don't need to define it in the main method. Just put the body of the roll method after the declaration. – Jashaszun Oct 27 '16 at 23:19
